NORTHSTAR RETAIL CO.
Learning and Blocker Journal — Live Inventory Sync Mini Prototype

Purpose: keep the support tool’s “is this in stock?” answers accurate and current.

1. Prototype Scope
The prototype uses one TypeScript/Fastify application and one PostgreSQL database. Its learning objective is to demonstrate inventory synchronization, transactional consistency, stale/duplicate update protection, and background processing through a small transactional outbox.
GET /inventory/:sku — support-tool inventory lookup.
POST /sync/inventory — simulated upstream inventory update.
inventory table — current local inventory read model.
outbox table — durable inventory-change events.
sourceVersion — prevents an older event from overwriting a newer state.
worker.ts — prototype downstream/support-tool integration point.

2. Resources Consulted
Resource | What was learned/checked | Location
TypeScript TSConfig — module | Confirmed that modern Node.js projects can use NodeNext and that module choice affects module resolution. | https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig/module
TypeScript TSConfig — moduleResolution | Confirmed NodeNext resolution behavior and contrasted it with bundler resolution; useful for diagnosing local .js import errors. | https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig/moduleResolution.html
TypeScript Modules Reference | Confirmed how package.json "type": "module" affects .ts/.js module format under NodeNext and why emitted JavaScript matters. | https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/modules/reference.html
Fastify TypeScript documentation | Confirmed Fastify TypeScript setup, the need for @types/node, and generic typing for request/reply. | https://fastify.dev/docs/latest/Reference/TypeScript/
Fastify Getting Started | Confirmed the basic Fastify application/startup pattern used by the prototype. | https://fastify.dev/docs/v5.11.x/Guides/Getting-Started/
Project source files | The prototype’s package.json, tsconfig.json, server.ts, db.ts, sync.ts, worker.ts and schema.sql were used as the primary implementation reference. | Local project files
node-postgres Pool documentation | Confirmed Pool/pool.connect usage and the rule that a single checked-out client must be used for a transaction; this matches sync.ts. | https://node-postgres.com/apis/pool
Zod documentation | Confirmed Zod's TypeScript-first schema validation and parse-based validation used for the inventory request payload. | 

3. Error and Blocker Log
File | Location | Observed error | Diagnosis | Troubleshooting action
tsconfig.json | 1:1 | Unable to load schema from https://json.schemastore.org/tsconfig: Failed to fetch. (json-65536) | Editor could not fetch the remote JSON schema. This is an editor/network/schema-service warning, not evidence that tsconfig.json is syntactically invalid. | Treat as non-blocking if TypeScript type-checking works. If desired, disable remote JSON schema validation or use a locally cached schema.
worker.ts | 1:22 | Cannot find module './db.js' or its corresponding type declarations. (typescript-2307) | The editor/compiler could not resolve the local module. The source file is db.ts while the import uses the Node ESM-style .js specifier; resolution also depends on the project being recognized as a NodeNext/ESM project and dependencies/config being installed correctly. | Verify package.json has "type": "module"; tsconfig uses NodeNext; run npm install; ensure db.ts exists; run tsc from the project root. If the mobile IDE still cannot resolve .js-to-.ts substitution, build with tsc or simplify the demo to CommonJS/bundler resolution.
server.ts | 1:21 | Cannot find module 'fastify' or its corresponding type declarations. (typescript-2307) | Fastify package/type declarations were not available to the TypeScript language service. | Run npm install from the project root and confirm fastify exists in dependencies. Then restart the TypeScript server/editor.
server.ts | 2:18 | Cannot find module '@fastify/cors' or its corresponding type declarations. (typescript-2307) | CORS dependency was unavailable to the editor/compiler. | Run npm install and confirm @fastify/cors is installed. Restart/reload the editor if diagnostics remain.
server.ts | 3:19 | Cannot find module 'zod' or its corresponding type declarations. (typescript-2307) | Zod dependency was unavailable to the editor/compiler. | Run npm install and confirm zod is installed.
server.ts | 5:22 | Cannot find module './db.js' or its corresponding type declarations. (typescript-2307) | Same local ESM/module-resolution issue seen in worker.ts. | Verify project root, package.json type/module settings, db.ts presence, and TypeScript version; then re-run typecheck.
server.ts | 8:8 | Cannot find module './sync.js' or its corresponding type declarations. (typescript-2307) | Same local ESM/module-resolution issue; sync.ts exists but the editor cannot resolve the .js specifier. | Verify NodeNext configuration and build/typecheck from project root.
server.ts | 11:8 | Cannot find module './worker.js' or its corresponding type declarations. (typescript-2307) | Same local ESM/module-resolution issue. | Verify NodeNext configuration and build/typecheck from project root.
server.ts | 13:1 | All imports in import declaration are unused. (typescript-6192) | Likely a cascading diagnostic caused by unresolved modules/import analysis rather than the intended final code. | Resolve the module/dependency errors first; re-run typecheck before removing imports.
server.ts | 41:35 / 41:44 | Parameter 'request'/'reply' implicitly has an 'any' type. (typescript-7044) | Fastify route handler types were not being inferred correctly, most likely because Fastify itself was unresolved. Fastify also supports explicit route generics for request/reply typing. | Install/resolve Fastify first. If diagnostics persist, explicitly type route generics (Params/Body/Reply) as recommended by Fastify's TypeScript docs.
server.ts | 106:36 / 106:45 | Parameter 'request'/'reply' implicitly has an 'any' type. (typescript-7044) | Same Fastify type-inference cascade as above. | Resolve Fastify/@types/node first; add explicit route types if needed.
server.ts | 151:3 and subsequent locations | Cannot find name 'process'. Do you need to install type definitions for node? (typescript-2580) | Node.js global types were unavailable to TypeScript. | Install dev dependency @types/node and ensure tsconfig includes the normal Node type environment. Fastify's TypeScript guide explicitly calls out @types/node.

4. Troubleshooting Process Followed
Step 1 — Classify the diagnostics: Separate warnings from blocking TypeScript errors. The JSON schema fetch failure was classified as an editor/network warning. Module-resolution, missing Node types, and implicit-any diagnostics were treated as actual blockers.
Step 2 — Check dependency availability: The repeated errors for fastify, @fastify/cors and zod indicate that the editor could not see installed packages. The first corrective action is npm install from the project root, followed by checking node_modules and package-lock.json.
Step 3 — Check Node type definitions: The process error points directly to missing Node.js type declarations. Confirm @types/node is installed as a dev dependency and reload/restart the TypeScript language service.
Step 4 — Check module system consistency: The local ./db.js, ./sync.js and ./worker.js errors were investigated against the chosen ESM/NodeNext design. package.json should contain "type": "module" and tsconfig should use NodeNext. The imports are .js because emitted JavaScript will be .js; TypeScript's NodeNext mode is responsible for resolving the source during compilation.
Step 5 — Re-run the compiler instead of relying only on editor diagnostics: Run npm run typecheck from the project root. This distinguishes an editor-language-service problem from a real compiler problem.
Step 6 — Address cascading diagnostics last: The unused-import and implicit-any messages should be re-evaluated after package/module errors are fixed. Missing Fastify types can cause request/reply inference to degrade.
Step 7 — Validate the runtime path: After typecheck succeeds, start PostgreSQL, initialize schema.sql, start the Fastify app, call /health, query a SKU, then post a newer and an older inventory event.

5. Validation Test Sequence
Test | Action | Expected result
Health | GET /health | Returns service status = ok.
Initial stock | GET /inventory/NS-1001 | Returns quantity 24 and inStock true.
New update | POST /sync/inventory with sourceVersion 101 and quantity 3 | Update is applied and an InventoryChanged outbox event is created.
Stale update | POST the same SKU with sourceVersion 100 and quantity 99 | Update is ignored as stale_or_duplicate; quantity remains 3.
Outbox | Observe worker console | InventoryChanged event is picked up and marked processed.

6. Learning Outcomes
A live inventory service is primarily a consistency and ordering problem, not a high-scale infrastructure problem for a few thousand SKUs.
The transactional outbox pattern keeps the inventory update and downstream event creation atomic.
sourceVersion provides a simple ordering/idempotency guard against delayed or duplicate upstream events.
Strict TypeScript is useful, but missing dependencies and module configuration can produce cascading diagnostics that must be solved from the root cause outward.
NodeNext/ESM requires deliberate consistency between package.json, tsconfig.json, source imports and emitted JavaScript.
Fastify supports explicit request/reply generics when inference is insufficient; @types/node is a required part of a normal Fastify TypeScript setup.
For a learning prototype, PostgreSQL can serve both as the inventory database and as the durable outbox store, avoiding unnecessary Redis/Kafka infrastructure.

7. Current Blocker Status
Primary blocker: the development environment shown in the screenshots is not successfully resolving the project's npm dependencies, Node.js type definitions, and/or local ESM modules. The screenshots do not prove that the application logic itself is wrong. The next gate is a clean `npm install` followed by `npm run typecheck` from the project root. Only after that should runtime/database troubleshooting proceed.
Secondary/non-blocking issue: tsconfig.json cannot fetch the SchemaStore URL. This is an editor/schema-service connectivity issue and should not be confused with a TypeScript compilation failure.

8. Recommended Recovery Checklist
Confirm Node.js and npm are installed and available in the terminal.
Open the terminal at the directory containing package.json.
Run npm install.
Confirm node_modules/fastify, node_modules/@fastify/cors, node_modules/zod and node_modules/@types/node exist.
Confirm package.json contains "type": "module".
Confirm tsconfig.json contains module = NodeNext and moduleResolution = NodeNext.
Confirm src/db.ts, src/sync.ts and src/worker.ts exist.
Run npm run typecheck.
If module errors persist, restart/reload the editor's TypeScript language service.
Only after typecheck succeeds, start PostgreSQL and run schema.sql.
Start the service and execute the validation sequence above.

